---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

// The canonical Creator Store identity for "Apple for Studio". The asset id has exactly ONE
// definition in this repository — packages/shared/src/index.ts — and everything, including this
// page, derives from it. Do not restate the number here; a second copy is a second thing to
// forget to change. (Why create.roblox.com/store/asset: roblox.com/library/<id> 307-redirects
// there, so Roblox itself names that the canonical URL.)
import {
  STUDIO_PLUGIN_ASSET_ID as ASSET_ID,
  STUDIO_PLUGIN_URL as STORE_URL,
  STUDIO_PLUGIN_STORE_LIVE as storeLive,
  STUDIO_PLUGIN_STORE_REFUSAL as refusal,
} from '../../lib/studio-plugin';

// This page used to read "Roblox removed the previous plugin listing (<ASSET_ID>)", which was two
// wrong things in one sentence. ASSET_ID is the CURRENT listing, not a previous one — the previous
// one was a different id on a different account and is not named here. And "removed" was written
// before anyone had seen a removal notice; what had been seen was a dashboard banner saying the
// asset "may be in violation". It has since become literally true, which is luck, not rigour.
//
// So the wording now derives from what Roblox actually says about THIS id on its appeals page,
// through one shared constant. If the appeal succeeds, `refusal` goes null and this paragraph
// disappears on its own rather than needing a reader to notice it is stale.
/*
 * THE BODY WAS GATED AND THE METADATA WAS NOT, WHICH IS THE HALF PEOPLE ACTUALLY SHARE.
 *
 * A <meta name="description"> and its og:description twin are what a search result and a pasted
 * link show. This page's body has asked STUDIO_PLUGIN_STORE_LIVE since the listing was removed,
 * while its description went on promising a Creator Store install path in every snippet — so the
 * promise reached the reader BEFORE the correction did, and the correction only arrived if they
 * clicked. Same constant, same answer, one sentence earlier.
 */
const decided = refusal
  ? new Date(refusal.decidedAt).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric' })
  : null;

// `storeLive` — whether the asset is actually distributable — is imported above rather than
// declared here. It used to be a local `const storeLive = false`, which made this page the only
// surface that knew the plugin was unpublished while the landing, dashboard and pairing dialog
// all linked straight to the store regardless. One definition, in packages/shared, now gates all
// of them; re-probe and flip it there. Rationale and the probe command live with the constant.
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Install the plugin — Apple Docs"
  description={storeLive
    ? 'How to install Apple for Studio from the Roblox Creator Store, and what Studio asks for the first time it runs.'
    : 'Public installation of Apple for Studio is unavailable: Roblox removed the Creator Store listing and an appeal is pending. What the decision means, and what the install steps will be once a listing exists.'}
  heading="Install Apple for Studio"
>
  <p>
    Apple builds inside Roblox Studio through a plugin. The plugin is the product's hands: it
    reads and edits scripts, creates instances, sets properties, reads the
    output — always inside the one place you connect it to, and always wrapped in a Studio undo
    waypoint.
  </p>

  <p>
    There is one supported way to get it: <strong>the Roblox Creator Store</strong>.
  </p>

  {
    storeLive ? (
      <p class="cta-row">
        <a href={STORE_URL} class="btn btn-primary" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">
          Get Apple on the Creator Store
        </a>
        <span>Free. Opens Roblox's site in a new tab.</span>
      </p>
    ) : (
      <div class="callout">
        {
          refusal ? (
            <p>
              <strong>Public installation is unavailable, and it is a moderation decision.</strong>{' '}
              Roblox removed this plugin's Creator Store listing (<code>{ASSET_ID}</code>) on {decided},
              giving the reason <strong>“{refusal.reason}”</strong>. The store URL is not a working
              install path, and the “Distribute on Creator Store” setting is locked rather than merely
              switched off — so this is not a step waiting to be taken.
              {refusal.appealId ? ' An appeal has been sent and is awaiting a decision.' : ' An appeal has not been sent.'}
              {' '}The instructions below apply only if and when an approved listing exists. No release
              date is confirmed, and none can be promised while the decision stands.
            </p>
          ) : (
            <p>
              <strong>Public installation is unavailable.</strong> The plugin
              (<code>{ASSET_ID}</code>) is not distributable on the Creator Store today, so its store
              URL is not a working install path. The instructions below apply only once an approved
              listing exists. No release date is confirmed.
            </p>
          )
        }
      </div>
    )
  }

  <h2>{storeLive ? 'Installing' : 'After public installation opens'}</h2>
  <p>
    Roblox splits plugin installation into two halves, and the second half happens inside Studio.
    A web page cannot install a plugin into Studio — there is no one-click hand-off, no matter
    what the button on the store page looks like.
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Acquire it on the web.</strong> Open the Creator Store page for Apple and click the
      blue button at the top right — for a free plugin it reads <strong>Get Plugin</strong>. Per
      Roblox's own documentation this only "add[s] it to your inventory". Nothing has reached
      Studio yet.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Install it in Studio.</strong> Open Roblox Studio, open the <strong>Toolbox</strong>
      (Studio's <strong>View</strong> menu, or the <strong>Home</strong> tab), and find Apple —
      under <strong>Inventory</strong> after step 1, or by searching the
      <strong>Creator Store</strong> tab. Click it, then press <strong>Install</strong>. Roblox's
      documentation is explicit that this is the step that adds a plugin "to the mezzanine's
      <strong>Plugins</strong> tab".
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Check it landed.</strong> A <strong>Apple</strong> button appears in Studio's
      <strong>Plugins</strong> tab. Clicking it docks the Apple panel.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Studio will ask permission to reach the network.</strong> Apple talks to its server
      over HTTPS. Roblox documents that "the first time a plugin attempts to use the service, the
      user might be prompted to give the plugin permission to communicate with the particular web
      address", and that these permissions can be accepted, denied and revoked at any time in the
      <strong>Plugin Management</strong> window. Apple does nothing until you allow it. This is
      not the same setting as <em>Game Settings → Security → Allow HTTP Requests</em>, which
      governs your game's own code, not plugins.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Next: connect it to a project</h2>
  <p>
    Installing the plugin does not link it to anything. A freshly installed Apple panel says
    <strong>Not connected</strong> and will not touch your place until you pair it with one Apple
    project — see <a href="/docs/connect">Connect a project</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What it can and cannot do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Can:</strong> read and edit scripts, create, move and delete instances, set
      properties, snapshot the instance tree, read output logs, move the
      camera — inside the place you connected, and only while Studio is open with that place.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cannot:</strong> read files on your computer, act on places you did not connect,
      run while Studio is closed, or publish your game. Publishing stays a human decision made
      with your own Roblox account.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Refuses by name:</strong> it will not start or stop play mode — you run and stop
      tests yourself — and it will not execute code it was sent, load a remote asset you do not
      own, or insert an asset carrying a script. Each refusal comes back with its reason rather
      than failing quietly, and each one costs the assistant tools it would otherwise offer.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Removing it</h2>
  <p>
    Uninstall Apple from Studio's plugin management window, the same place you manage every other
    plugin. You can also stop Apple acting on a place at any moment without uninstalling anything
    — press <strong>Disconnect</strong> in the panel, or disconnect from the web workspace. See
    <a href="/docs/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--violet">
    <p>
      <strong>Developer note — not the normal path.</strong> If you are working on Apple itself,
      you can build the plugin from source and load it as a local plugin. That path is documented
      separately in <a href="/docs/build-from-source">Build the plugin from source</a>. It is for
      development and debugging only; do not follow it to use Apple.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>

<style>
  .cta-row {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.9rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 1.75rem;
  }

  .cta-row span {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }
</style>
